  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nRest of Today-Saturday...Hot and humid once again across east\ncentral Florida this afternoon, with limited shower coverage as of\n2 PM and a delayed, weak east coast sea breeze. Widely scattered\nshowers and storms are forecast to develop along the sea breeze as\nit moves inland through this evening, with PoPs 20-40%. However,\nthis may be a bit overdone given the weak cu field seen on visible\nsatellite imagery. Regardless, most areas will remain dry today,\nwith little relief from scorching heat indices 108-112 degrees. A\nHeat Advisory remains in effect through 8 PM. Any storms that\nmanage to develop today will be capable of strong wind gusts to 50\nmph, with dry air in the mid levels. Convection is forecast to\ntaper off into this evening over the interior, with overnight lows\nin the mid to upper 70s.\n\nRinse and repeat to begin the weekend, as high pressure prevails\nover the Florida peninsula. Below average coverage of showers and\nstorms continues, with PoPs generally 20-40%. A wave of Saharan\nDust is forecast to move into the area through Saturday,\nincreasing the chances for a few strong storms, with wind gusts to\naround 50 mph. Light offshore flow becomes S/SE along the coast as\nthe sea breeze develops and moves inland. Overall, winds remain\naround 10 mph or less. Low precipitation coverage and a weak sea\nbreeze will continue to allow highs to reach well into the 90s.\nMeanwhile, humid conditions produce heat indices 108-112 degrees.\nAdditional Heat Advisories are likely through at least Saturday.\nDon't forget to integrate heat safety into your weekend plans!\n\nSunday-Thursday...Below normal rainfall chances, well below normal\nat times, persist into early next week. Surface high pressure \nbuilds back towards the peninsula and into the eastern Gulf\nthrough the period. Aloft, a strong upper level ridge flattens \nover the Southeast US. Overall, little change to the pattern for \neast central Florida. Forecast PoPs peak at 20-30% or less, with \ndry conditions expected area-wide for at least Monday. Most areas\nwill likely remain dry. Regardless, strong storms will be \npossible each afternoon, with waves of SAL supporting wind gusts \nto 50 mph in storms that can survive the overall subsidence. Light\noffshore flow will continue to oppose the afternoon sea breeze, \nwhich remains weak at around 10 mph or less. Highs in the mid-90s \nwill creep towards the upper 90s at times, with humid conditions \ncontinuing. Additional Heat Advisories will need to be considered \nthrough the foreseeable future.",
